GAP9: a hardware loop of one OffsetedWritePtr store gets its nop pad

GAP9 silicon runs a hardware loop whose body is one instruction once,
whatever its count (gap-llvm-toolchain ROADMAP §5.7), so the hardware-loop
pass pads a body shorter than two instructions (-mhwloopmin, default 2)
with a nop. It counts the body by the insns' length attribute
(length_for_loop).

__builtin_pulp_OffsetedWritePtr (the SDK's GAP_WRITE_PTR, as in
eu_sem_inc) and __builtin_pulp_event_unit_read_fenced (evt_read32) expand
to sync.md's *memory_barrier followed by the store or load. The barrier
prints nothing but has the default length, 4, so a loop of one such store
counted as two instructions and got no pad:

  void f(unsigned b) { for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
      __builtin_pulp_OffsetedWritePtr((int *)1, (int *)b, 1028); }

became `lp.setupi x1,100,(.L5)` over the lone p.sw at -O2, -O3 and -Os,
which stores once. length_for_loop now leaves the barrier out, so that loop
counts one instruction and gets the nop.

Only the hardware-loop count changes. A length of 0 on the barrier itself
would do it too, but the length also sizes blocks for basic-block reordering:
the SDK's kernels, which end with gap_waitbarrier's barrier and event-unit
load, then got their epilogue copied instead of jumped to (119 objects of the
whole-SDK objdump). A body of one of these stores or loads and anything else
was already two or more instructions; a longer one counts one instruction
less per barrier, which only matters for the lp.setupi (up to 15
instructions) or lp.setup choice.

--- a/gcc/config/riscv/riscv.c
+++ b/gcc/config/riscv/riscv.c
@@ -6279,11 +6279,29 @@ void riscv_hardware_loop (void)
   cfun->machine->has_hardware_loops++;
 }
 
+/* True if INSN is sync.md's *memory_barrier, which prints nothing: the
+   barrier that __builtin_pulp_OffsetedWritePtr and
+   __builtin_pulp_event_unit_read_fenced put before their access.  */
+
+static bool riscv_codeless_barrier_p (rtx_insn *insn)
+{
+  rtx pat = PATTERN (insn);
+
+  return (GET_CODE (pat) == SET
+	  && GET_CODE (SET_SRC (pat)) == UNSPEC
+	  && XINT (SET_SRC (pat), 1) == UNSPEC_MEMORY_BARRIER);
+}
+
+/* INSN's instructions in a hardware loop's body.  A code-less barrier has
+   none: a loop of one OffsetedWritePtr store is one instruction, which GAP9
+   runs once, and needs the pad.  */
+
 static int length_for_loop (rtx_insn *insn)
 {
   int length = 0;
 
-  if (NONDEBUG_INSN_P (insn)) length += (get_attr_length (insn))/4;
+  if (NONDEBUG_INSN_P (insn) && !riscv_codeless_barrier_p (insn))
+    length += (get_attr_length (insn))/4;
   return length;
 }
 
